                  exemplar: "Talk for Learning Approaches (TfL):\n• Through teamwork and collaborative communication skills, learners in mixed ability groups explore the use of independent and dependent clauses in varied contexts.\n• Learners watch YouTube videos for more examples.\n• Dependent clause is a part of a sentence that has subject and a verb but cannot stand alone as a complete thought.\n• E.g.: Manu dropped the bottle because his hands were full.\n• Dependent clauses are introduced with subordinating conjunctions such as: because, before, though, while, as, if, as if, who, that, where etc.\n\nProject-Based Learning:\n• Learners watch a recorded version of a renowned TV newscaster and apply in their mixed ability groups to construct thematic narratives/ speeches with dependent and independent clauses to suit varied contexts.\n• Independent clause (main) is a group of words that contains a subject and a verb and expresses a complete thought.\n• E.g., Kafui studied in the lab for his Chemistry test.\n\nNB: The Dependent (Subordinate) Clause depends on the main clause for completion and to make sense.",
